# Decisions log

Every tool choice and the reason, logged as it happens (CLAUDE.md rule 6).

| Date | Decision | Reason | Decided by |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2026-11-08 | Target site: blog.cloudflare.com; fallback www.smashingmagazine.com | Permissive robots.txt, sitemap index with lastmod on every entry, several posts per week. Smashing is the fallback; simonwillison.net rejected (sitemap has no lastmod). | Hakob (Claude recommended) |
| 2026-11-08 | Fallback switch rule | Task 1 verifies crawlability from GitHub Actions (robots allows, no bot challenge, sitemap reachable). If it fails, switch to fallback via config.yaml only and log the switch and reason here. | Hakob |
| 2026-11-08 | LLM: Google Gemini REST API (free tier), model gemini-2.5-flash, instead of Anthropic API | Free tier is enough for one short daily summary; called with requests, so no new SDK. Endpoint `v1beta/models/{model}:generateContent`, key from env GEMINI_API_KEY via X-goog-api-key header. Provider and model in config.yaml, pinned name (no "latest" alias). Output tokens capped. 429/timeouts fall back to the deterministic template summary. | Hakob |
| 2026-11-08 | Delivery time 09:00 Asia/Yerevan, cron `0 5 * * *` UTC | Team's morning; Armenia has no DST, so the UTC cron is stable. | Hakob |
| 2026-11-08 | PSI regressions need confirmation: `psi.regression_confirmations` (default 2) | PSI lab scores are noisy; first unconfirmed drop is reported as info "possible regression, awaiting confirmation". | Hakob |
| 2026-11-08 | All tools in PLAN.md section 5 approved with the plan (Python 3.12, requests, beautifulsoup4 + lxml, urllib.robotparser, PyYAML, Gemini REST API via requests, Telegram Bot API, GitHub Actions, pytest) | Approving the plan approves exactly that table; any other tool, service or dependency is asked separately (CLAUDE.md rule 7). | Hakob |
| 2026-11-08 | Exact pins in requirements.txt: requests 2.34.2, beautifulsoup4 4.15.0, lxml 6.1.3, PyYAML 6.0.3, pytest 9.2.0 | Latest releases at install time, tested with Python 3.12 (same as the workflow). Only the approved direct dependencies are pinned; no new packages added. | Claude (task 1) |
| 2026-11-08 | robots.txt handling: 200 = parse, 401/403 = disallow all, other 4xx = allow all, 5xx or network error = source failed and nothing is crawled | Common convention (also what urllib.robotparser.read() does), with the safe choice when robots.txt cannot be read. | Claude (task 1) |
| 2026-11-08 | Sitemap record status: ok = everything read, partial = some URLs but a child failed or a cap (max_urls / max_child_sitemaps) was hit, failed = root unreadable or no child readable. URLs stored per child sitemap as {loc: lastmod}. Child sitemaps fetched freshest first by the index's lastmod. | A diff can still compare child sitemaps that were complete on both days when the record is partial, and caps cut the oldest sitemaps, not the newest. No new dependency (lxml + stdlib gzip). | Claude (task 2) |
| 2026-11-08 | Snapshots: data/snapshots/YYYY-MM-DD/ (UTC day), compact JSON with sorted keys, atomic writes, meta.json written last with every source's status; same-day rerun overwrites; retention deletes only real-date folders directly inside snapshot_dir older than retention_days (no symlinks followed). snapshot_dir is resolved relative to config.yaml. | Stable, small diffs; a crashed run never leaves half a file; retention cannot delete anything outside the snapshot folder. | Claude (task 2) |
| 2026-11-08 | sitemap.max_urls 5000 -> 15000 | Dry run on GitHub Actions (run 42503018547) hit the 5000 cap: blog.cloudflare.com has about 10,200 sitemap URLs (posts 5 files, ~2000 each except posts-5 with 100; authors 1339; tags 776; archives 21). 15000 reads everything with headroom, so the sitemap is ok instead of partial every day. Cost: about 1 MB per daily sitemap.json. | Hakob (chose to set it from real counts) |
| 2026-11-08 | On-page crawl: homepage + crawler.extra_urls + newest crawler.max_pages sitemap URLs by lastmod (same host only, undated URLs after dated ones). Redirects followed by hand (max 10 hops, same host only), so every hop is robots-checked, rate-limited and recorded; off-host redirects recorded, not followed. Page status ok / failed / disallowed / skipped (budget); HTTP 4xx/5xx is status ok with the http_status recorded (the diff judges it). Internal links stored per page, capped at 1000 per page. HTML parsed with beautifulsoup4 + lxml, server HTML only. | Matches PLAN.md section 3; a redirect into a disallowed path is never fetched; the collector separates "could not collect" from "page is broken". No new dependency. | Claude (task 3) |
| 2026-11-08 | New config keys crawler.sitemap_include / crawler.sitemap_exclude (regexes on the URL path, used only when picking the newest sitemap URLs). Cloudflare config excludes ^/tag/, ^/author/, ^/[a-z]{2}-[a-z]{2}/ | First live crawl (run 42505789427): the 50 newest by lastmod were 17 tag, 12 author and 18 translated pages, only 5 English posts, because tag/author pages get a new lastmod with every post. Patterns live in config, so the code stays generic. | Hakob (Claude recommended) |
| 2026-11-08 | Link checker: input = internal_links of today's crawled HTTP 200 HTML pages; URLs the crawl already fetched reuse that result (no second request, not counted in the cap); the rest ordered most-linked first, then alphabetical, and the first links.max_links are checked. HEAD first, GET (stream, body not read) when HEAD gets a network error or any status >= 400, so servers that mishandle HEAD do not cause false broken links. Redirects followed by hand like the crawl (robots-checked hops, max 10, loop detection, off-host not followed unless check_external). Link status ok / failed / disallowed / skipped; 4xx/5xx is data (counts.broken). Record ok / partial (failed or skipped links, or crawl partial) / failed (crawl failed or nothing checkable) / disabled. Left-out-by-cap is counted, not a failure. linked_from capped at 20 pages per link, count exact. | Deterministic set each day so the diff compares like with like; robots.txt always obeyed; "could not check" kept apart from "link is broken". No new dependency. | Claude (task 4) |
| 2026-11-08 | links.max_links 300 -> 150 | At 1 request/s, 300 links take ~5 min on their own, but the crawl already uses ~1 min of the 5-min runtime budget, so ~70 links would be skipped (partial) every day and PSI (task 5) would have no time left. 150 links ~2.5 min. | Hakob (Claude recommended) |
| 2026-11-08 | PSI uses its own small client (psi.PsiClient, requests.Session), not the PoliteClient | PSI is Google's API, not the target site: robots.txt and the 1 request/s site rate limit do not apply. Own timeout (psi.timeout_seconds read, 10 s connect), psi.max_retries retries on 429/5xx/timeouts/connection errors (5 s backoff), no retry on 400/403. API key from env psi.api_key_env, sent as the documented `key` query parameter, never stored, and scrubbed from every error text. No key = PSI record failed "no API key", no request. Calls use category=performance only (faster). Keyless calls were tested: Google answers 429 (shared daily quota exhausted), so a key is required. | Claude (task 5) |
| 2026-11-08 | PSI runs in a background thread (stdlib threading, no new dependency) at the same time as robots/sitemap/crawl/links, with the same runtime.budget_seconds deadline. A PSI call or retry starts only if it can finish before the deadline even at psi.timeout_seconds; otherwise it is "skipped". New config check: psi.timeout_seconds < runtime.budget_seconds. | PSI calls are mostly waiting on Google (10-40 s each); the site collectors already use ~211 s of the 300 s. Running PSI first would cut into the crawl/link budget, running it last could not fit 2 calls at 60 s timeout. In parallel the run stays ~211 s. Lighthouse loading the homepage while we crawl is one extra page load on the site, acceptable for politeness. A PSI crash or hang is recorded as PSI failed and never loses the other sources. | Claude (task 5) |
| 2026-11-08 | PSI record shape: per URL x strategy run with status ok/failed/skipped, lab (performance_score 0-100, lcp_ms, cls, tbt_ms), field.url and field.origin (CrUX lcp_ms, inp_ms, cls with p75 percentile + category, overall_category) or null with field.notes saying "no field data"; CrUX origin_fallback data is stored under origin only, never as URL data. CLS field percentile divided by 100 (PSI reports x100). Lighthouse runtimeError = run failed with no lab numbers. Record ok / partial / failed / disabled. | The diff (task 6) can compare like with like and never mistakes origin data for page data. | Claude (task 5) |
| 2026-11-08 | URLs that differ only by a trailing slash are the same URL in the diff engine (rules.trailing_slash_equivalent: true). A canonical that differs from the page URL only by that slash is one grouped site-level info finding (canonical_slash_mismatch), not a per-page "canonical points elsewhere". | blog.cloudflare.com serves /x and /x/ with HTTP 200; the sitemap lists /x, the canonical is /x/. 50 identical canonical warnings would be noise, but the duplicate URL form is still a real minor issue worth one line. Configurable so another site can use strict matching. | Hakob (Claude recommended) |
| 2026-11-08 | Diff engine design (src/seo_reviewer/diff.py + rules.py): severities, check labels and suggested_next_step live in rules.py (one entry per rule id, never written by the LLM); config.yaml rules.severity_overrides can change a rule's severity. Findings are changes: an issue present on both days goes to "ongoing" (counts), not to findings, so a quiet day is "all stable". Baseline run (no earlier ok day) lists current issues with change "baseline". Pages new in the newest-N crawl window report their issues as change "new_page". More than rules.group_min (5) findings with the same rule and change become one grouped finding. status = baseline / issues (any critical or warning) / stable. | Keeps the daily review short (CLAUDE.md "avoid noise"), still says what changed and what got fixed (resolved = info), and keeps every next step deterministic. | Claude (task 6) |
| 2026-11-08 | Source gating: failed source = one finding (robots/sitemap/crawl critical, links/psi warning); a source whose upstream failed too (crawl <- robots, links <- crawl) gets an info "not run" finding instead. Partial = one info finding + like-for-like compare (sitemap partial: added URLs only, no removals). Baseline per source = snapshot.last_day_with_status(ok). A source that failed on the previous day and works today gets an info "recovered". | Exactly one finding per root cause; a missing sitemap child never looks like thousands of removed URLs. | Claude (task 6) |
| 2026-11-08 | PSI lab confirmation is tracked per URL x strategy over the ok runs in earlier snapshots (a failed run or day is skipped: it neither advances nor resets the counter). Reference = last run that was not regressed (or the run that confirmed). Regressed = score drop > psi_performance_drop_points, LCP/CLS/TBT worse than lab_tolerance. Pending = info "possible regression, awaiting confirmation (k of N)"; confirmed = warning, after which the new value is the reference (not repeated daily); a fine run after a pending one = info "cleared". CrUX compared directly with the previous ok run by category: FAST -> SLOW critical, any other worse step warning, better info; URL and origin data separate, origin once per strategy. --compare-to D makes PSI count from D. | Matches PLAN.md section 3; run-level history is the same "only compare ok data" rule at the granularity PSI records it. | Claude (task 6) |
| 2026-11-08 | New config keys: thresholds.sitemap_drop_percent (10: more removed in one day = warning instead of info) and the rules section (trailing_slash_equivalent, group_min 5, max_urls_per_finding 50, severity_overrides {}). | A mass removal is usually a broken sitemap build, not real deletions. | Claude (task 6) |
| 2026-11-08 | Report builder (src/seo_reviewer/report.py) writes delivery.report_dir/YYYY-MM-DD.md (relative to config.yaml, like snapshot_dir) from findings.json plus that day's snapshot only: meta.json for the run line, and short coverage counts copied from the source records (e.g. crawl '51 of 51 pages fetched', links 'checked, broken, over the cap') for the source health table. Three modes from findings.json status: baseline (says it is a baseline run, lists current issues), issues (source health table, findings by severity critical/warning/info in findings.json order, so regressions first), stable (short: headline, one source line, info findings as one-liners capped at 10, known-issue counts). A failed source is always named at the top with what was not compared ('PSI failed: ...; Core Web Vitals and PageSpeed scores not compared today.'). Grouped findings list their URLs (collapsed in a <details> block above 5). Known issues = findings.json ongoing counts. Text from the site is markdown-escaped; URLs are <autolinks>. | PLAN.md section 7 and CLAUDE.md data rules: every number is copied from data, next steps come from the rules, the quiet day stays short. Plain markdown renders on GitHub, where the Telegram message (task 9) links to. | Claude (task 7) |
| 2026-11-08 | main.py: the default run is now collect + diff + report. --diff-only still only rebuilds findings.json (report untouched); new --report-only rebuilds the report from an existing findings.json (no network). crawl-check.yml prints the report and adds it to the run summary page; nothing is committed. | Keeps each offline step separate and testable; the run summary shows the real report for review. | Claude (task 7) |
| 2026-11-08 | summarize.py: Gemini generateContent via requests (no SDK), X-goog-api-key header, model/endpoint/maxOutputTokens/thinkingBudget/timeout from config.yaml, temperature 0.2. The model gets a compact copy of findings.json (headline, counts, source status, up to 40 findings with id/severity/check/change/url/evidence/count and up to 3 example URLs per grouped finding; no suggested_next_step, no details). One retry on HTTP 5xx after 2 s; no retry on 429 or timeout. Key never stored; error texts scrubbed. | PLAN.md sections 2, 5 and 8. Small prompt keeps the free tier cheap; 5xx 'overloaded' is often momentary, while 429 quota and timeouts would not clear inside the 5-minute budget. | Claude (task 8) |
| 2026-11-08 | Summary validator: every URL in the Gemini text must be in the data sent to it (trailing slash ignored), every number (regex, thousands commas allowed) must be in that data, every finding id must exist, text not empty, not cut off (MAX_TOKENS), not blocked, at most 1500 characters. Numbers from the prompt instructions do not count. Any failure = deterministic template (headline, failed sources, top 3 findings in findings.json order), with the reason. Result in data/snapshots/DAY/summary.json (source gemini/template, reason, model, text, finding_ids, validation_errors, rejected_text, token usage incl. thought tokens, findings_sha256). | CLAUDE.md: the LLM only summarizes deterministic findings. findings_sha256 lets --report-only detect a summary left over from an older findings.json (then the template is shown). | Claude (task 8) |
| 2026-11-08 | Report: '## Summary' section right after the header says 'Written by Gemini (model) ... checked' or 'Template summary (no AI). Gemini not used: REASON'; footer says which part is AI-written. config.py rejects llm.provider other than gemini, a model containing 'latest', and an endpoint without {model}. Default run = collect + diff + summarize + report; --report-only reuses summary.json (no network). | Reader always knows what is AI text; config guards match PLAN.md (pinned model). | Claude (task 8) |
| 2026-11-08 | LLM model: live run 42518853117 got HTTP 404 'models/gemini-2.5-flash is no longer available to new users' (template fallback worked). config.yaml now proposes gemini-3.8-flash (the model Google's error names), thinking_level low, thinking_budget null, max_output_tokens 2048. New config key llm.thinking_level (Gemini 3+: minimal/low/medium/high) next to llm.thinking_budget (Gemini 2.5); only one may be set, neither = model default. | Gemini 3+ models use thinkingLevel instead of thinkingBudget and cannot turn thinking off ('minimal' is not supported on 3.8 Flash); thought tokens count against maxOutputTokens, so 800 could cut the answer off. Model switch confirmed by Hakob 2026-11-08 (PLAN.md had named gemini-2.5-flash). | Claude (task 8) |
| 2026-11-08 | Gemini call: llm.timeout_seconds 30 -> 60; a timeout is now retried once like a 5xx; main.py passes a deadline (start + runtime.budget_seconds), so a call only starts with at least 15 s left and its read timeout is cut to the time left. | Live runs 42519692526 (HTTP 503 'high demand' twice) and 42520067334 (timed out after 30 s) on gemini-3.8-flash; the collect step takes ~210 s, so the call has ~90 s and must not push the run past the 5-minute budget. | Claude (task 8) |
